WEBVTT

1
00:00:02.700 --> 00:00:05.497
A Monday morning at the payments company.

2
00:00:05.497 --> 00:00:14.639
Overnight, one account, A-3101, made a run of odd payments, and the fraud team, the people who look for cheating, flagged it at 09:12.

3
00:00:14.767 --> 00:00:22.242
Their question: which other accounts share a phone or a laptop with this one, directly or through up to two other accounts?

4
00:00:22.242 --> 00:00:24.977
People who cheat together often share phones.

5
00:00:25.133 --> 00:00:26.311
Easy, you'd think.

6
00:00:26.311 --> 00:00:31.608
Put the links in a table, index every column, and each lookup takes microseconds.

7
00:00:31.608 --> 00:00:34.028
Let's see what one step really costs.

8
00:00:34.167 --> 00:00:39.020
The table has one row per link: this account used this phone or device.

9
00:00:39.020 --> 00:00:41.071
Call either one an identifier.

10
00:00:41.200 --> 00:00:47.009
Each column has an index, a sorted list of its values, like the index at the back of a book.

11
00:00:47.200 --> 00:00:50.356
One step crosses the table twice.

12
00:00:50.356 --> 00:00:57.816
Look up A-3101's rows through the account index: device D-77 and phone P-0177.

13
00:00:57.967 --> 00:01:02.067
Then look up each identifier's other rows: A-3102, twice.

14
00:01:02.067 --> 00:01:04.657
That's the table joined with itself.

15
00:01:04.800 --> 00:01:08.585
Two steps is four trips through the table, three steps is six.

16
00:01:08.585 --> 00:01:11.149
The query grows with every step you allow.

17
00:01:11.333 --> 00:01:12.519
So does the work.

18
00:01:12.519 --> 00:01:18.098
Say each account has 3 identifiers, and each identifier is shared by 2 accounts.

19
00:01:18.098 --> 00:01:21.166
Those are made-up numbers, to see the shape.

20
00:01:21.166 --> 00:01:23.328
Every step fans out by about 6.

21
00:01:23.500 --> 00:01:26.012
One step touches 6 rows.

22
00:01:26.012 --> 00:01:27.792
Three steps, 216.

23
00:01:27.792 --> 00:01:30.304
Six steps, about 47,000.

24
00:01:30.467 --> 00:01:37.300
And the database has to guess the size of each join before it runs, and its guesses get worse with every join.

25
00:01:37.467 --> 00:01:40.187
Now store the same links the other way round.

26
00:01:40.187 --> 00:01:48.168
Think of a detective's pinboard: a photo for each account and each phone, and a string between each account and every phone it used.

27
00:01:48.267 --> 00:01:51.704
To take one step, put your finger on A-3101's photo.

28
00:01:51.704 --> 00:01:53.885
Follow its strings to its phones.

29
00:01:53.885 --> 00:01:57.389
Then follow each phone's strings to the other photos.

30
00:01:57.389 --> 00:01:59.173
You never search the board.

31
00:01:59.333 --> 00:02:01.912
That's what a graph database stores.

32
00:02:01.912 --> 00:02:05.136
Neo4j is a database program built for graphs.

33
00:02:05.136 --> 00:02:06.640
Each photo is a node.

34
00:02:06.640 --> 00:02:08.789
Each string is a relationship.

35
00:02:08.933 --> 00:02:15.319
A node's record leads to its relationships, and each relationship records the nodes at both ends.

36
00:02:15.319 --> 00:02:22.429
So following one is a pointer read: the record says where the next record is, and Neo4j goes straight there.

37
00:02:22.533 --> 00:02:28.292
Neo4j calls this index-free adjacency: finding a node's neighbours needs no index.

38
00:02:28.292 --> 00:02:31.592
You use an index once, to find the start photo.

39
00:02:31.592 --> 00:02:36.719
After that, the cost of a step doesn't depend on how many accounts exist.

40
00:02:36.867 --> 00:02:38.260
So let's walk.

41
00:02:38.260 --> 00:02:45.328
Step one: two strings out, to D-77 and P-0177, and both lead to A-3102.

42
00:02:45.328 --> 00:02:47.617
Two paths, one account.

43
00:02:47.733 --> 00:02:52.393
Step two: from A-3102, phone P-0199 leads to A-3103.

44
00:02:52.393 --> 00:02:57.859
Still two paths, because there were two ways to reach A-3102.

45
00:02:57.967 --> 00:02:59.040
Now step three.

46
00:02:59.040 --> 00:03:02.047
How many paths, and how many new accounts?

47
00:03:02.047 --> 00:03:03.192
Pause and guess.

48
00:03:06.433 --> 00:03:09.862
Fourteen paths, and six accounts.

49
00:03:09.862 --> 00:03:15.577
A-3104 and A-3105 through device D-81 and phone P-0181.

50
00:03:15.733 --> 00:03:22.567
And four customers through a store kiosk, a shared screen in a shop, that A-3103 also used.

51
00:03:22.733 --> 00:03:24.820
The kiosk didn't find a fraud ring.

52
00:03:24.820 --> 00:03:27.504
It found four people who signed in at a shop.

53
00:03:27.667 --> 00:03:29.679
So the fan-out doesn't go away.

54
00:03:29.679 --> 00:03:32.601
Both designs pay for every path they explore.

55
00:03:32.601 --> 00:03:38.119
What changes is the price of each step, and how easily you can say up to three steps.

56
00:03:38.267 --> 00:03:39.983
So when should you use which?

57
00:03:39.983 --> 00:03:44.361
If your questions are one or two steps of a known shape, stay with tables.

58
00:03:44.361 --> 00:03:48.740
When they're paths of unknown length, rings or cycles, a graph store fits.

59
00:03:48.867 --> 00:03:50.602
And the strings aren't free.

60
00:03:50.602 --> 00:03:58.843
Every new one has to be tied into the lists of both its ends, and a total over all payments reads records scattered across the board.

61
00:03:58.967 --> 00:04:00.935
So, back to the flagged account.

62
00:04:00.935 --> 00:04:05.424
In tables, every step through a shared phone cost two lookups and a join.

63
00:04:05.424 --> 00:04:08.437
On the board, it's strings followed from a photo.

64
00:04:08.600 --> 00:04:17.353
A graph store answers by walking: find the start node through an index, follow relationships outward, and pay only for what you touch.

65
00:04:17.833 --> 00:04:27.568
In Neo4j's query language, Cypher, you even draw the step: an account, an arrow to an identifier, and an arrow back from another account.

66
00:04:27.733 --> 00:04:28.942
One quick check.

67
00:04:28.942 --> 00:04:35.888
Keep the same strings around A-3101, but put 13 million accounts on the board instead of 13.

68
00:04:35.888 --> 00:04:38.456
How many paths does step one find?

69
00:04:38.456 --> 00:04:39.664
Pause and think.

70
00:04:42.867 --> 00:04:43.549
Still two.

71
00:04:43.549 --> 00:04:47.851
A step costs the strings you follow, not the size of the board.
